<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Socrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–399 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Socrates was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing himself but questioned his fellow citizens about justice, virtue and knowledge, a method of inquiry known through the dialogues of his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>. In 399 BCE he was tried for impiety and corrupting the young, condemned and executed by drinking hemlock. He is regarded as a founder of Western philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="Socrates" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Lysippos (?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
